\textbf{Drug Discovery}
The integration of machine learning and chemoinformatics has significantly advanced computational drug discovery, driving the growth of deep learning and the emergence of meta-learning. This section outlines how meta-learning can be applied to accelerate drug discovery, a critical field with extensive implications. Drug repurposing involves a specific form of meta-learning where pre-trained deep learning models are fine-tuned for individual drugs. Similarly, predicting drug interactions can be categorized as a learning-to-learn problem, where a generic model is trained on related drugs and subsequently fine-tuned for specific instances. This approach is particularly relevant in high-throughput drug screening, enabling the rapid prediction of a drug's efficacy across diverse cell types. By leveraging a generalizable model, researchers can minimize experimental time and costs.

Our repository includes an implementation of a simple multi-layer perceptron (MLP) and drug similarity networks, achieving an accuracy of approximately 80\%. Additionally, we explore applying meta-learning to feature selection in drug discovery, demonstrating an accuracy ranging from 70\% to 90\%. This approach utilizes meta-learning to identify the most pertinent features for specific drugs, thereby enhancing model performance. For a detailed examination, please refer to https://github.com/andrewyeh2000/UCI-MLR.

\textbf{Future Work}
Future efforts should prioritize the application of meta-learning to large-scale, real-world datasets, such as those found in Electronic Health Records (EHR). Furthermore, there is a significant opportunity to apply these learning-to-learn techniques to graph neural networks, given that many biological networks are structured in similar ways. Finally, investigating data augmentation strategies within the framework of meta-learning could yield substantial advancements in this area.

